Types of consistency :
1.Strong consistency
2.Eventual consistency
3.Casual consistency
4.Weak consistency


Strong consistency - means system is strictly consistent and it gurantees that every read operation will definately be consistent and how it maintain this

4 nodes
read -> N1  N2
        N3  N4

when a read operation happens on N1 eg so it confirms from all other nodes n2,n3,n4 and then will get the result
when write happens so it will write on all and the provide result


Eventual consistency -

when a write happens  

write ->    N1  N2
            N3  N4
so it startes replicating that to other nodes also, like n2 then n3 then n4
so while replicating for a small period of time it can be unconsistenc but eventually it will become consistent.


Casual consistency -

if someone concurently making edits on different sections of a document.

it just ays that if an event depends on another event then everyone must see them in that order.
but unrelated events can appear in different orders for different users.



Weak consistency - 

SO its like when when update a data so its not gurantee when other users will see it or which user will see the updated data first and which will not. eg i updated my profile pic , some users seeing my updated image but few other are siing my old dp only.

It’s commonly acceptable for things like live video streams, gaming state, counters, or some cached data, where temporary outdated data is not critical.




Trade Off's that happens :

1.Scalalbility vs Performance -

Scalalbility is like increasing size and performance is like the speed and how well it executes a task 

so when you are increasing a system so obviously the performance will get affected.
as earlier e had lets say 1 TB of data and system is taking sometime to query now when the data becomes 2 TB the system will be slightly slower and performance will get affected.

2.Vertical Scaling vs Horizontal Scaling -

vert scal - inc cost and single point of failure can happen

hor scaling - it is complex, it requires maintenance

we discussed this in CAP theorem also

3.Consistency vs Availability -

cons - we have consistent data at all point of time

avail - system is available


so if a node goes down so either we have to take a downtime to get the server up and get consistent data

or
we have to jeopradize consistency and choose the availability of system

4.Latency vs Throughput - 

Latency - Time taken by a system to respond to some request.

Throughput - rate at which system can process or handle a workload.

so lets say we have 10 requets and our system is able to handle 10 requests/second
so this is our throughput

how many requests a system is able to handle in a given amount of time.


trade offs 
1.either you can have low latency(lower the time) and high throughput (more num og req getting)
2.We can have high throughput and higher latency




